"question_text": "In a standard fireworks display, when is the safest and most appropriate time to insert the electric match into the quick match leader of an aerial shell?",
"option_a": "At the manufacturer's factory before shipping",
"option_b": "In the hotel room the night before the show",
"option_c": "At the display site, ideally after the shell has been safely seated in the mortar",
"option_d": "While the shell is being transported in the truck",
"correct_answer": "C",
"explanation": "Shells should never be transported or heavily handled while fully armed with an e-match (unless specifically classified as such under DOT rules). Inserting the match at the site, preferably once the shell is in the tube, minimizes the risk of friction or static ignition during handling."
},
{
"module_name": "Module 3: Display Operations",
"question_text": "When testing a cue on a firing panel, the continuity light fails to illuminate (or the system reads infinite resistance/Open Circuit). What does this indicate?",
"option_a": "The circuit is perfect and ready to fire",
"option_b": "There is a short circuit in the wiring",
"option_c": "The electrical path is broken; either a wire is cut, a splice has come undone, or the e-match is defective/disconnected",
"option_d": "The battery in the firing panel is overcharged",
"correct_answer": "C",
"explanation": "An open circuit means the electrical loop is broken. Current cannot flow from the panel, through the e-match, and back to the panel, so the cue will fail to fire."
},
{
"module_name": "Module 3: Display Operations",
"question_text": "If a firing system with an Ohm meter reads 0.0 Ohms (near-zero resistance) on a cue that is connected to 100 feet of wire and an e-match, what is the most likely problem?",
"option_a": "The circuit is perfectly optimized",
"option_b": "The battery is dead",
"option_c": "There is a short circuit; the two wires are touching each other before the current reaches the e-match, meaning the match will not fire",
"option_d": "The e-match is excessively sensitive",
"correct_answer": "C",
"explanation": "An e-match and a long run of wire possess natural electrical resistance (usually several Ohms). A reading of zero means the current is finding a shortcut back to the panel before it ever hits the match head."
},
{
"module_name": "Module 3: Display Operations",
"question_text": "A technician wires 5 electric matches in a 'series' circuit on a single cue. If one of those 5 matches has a broken bridge wire (defective factory match), what will happen when the cue is fired?",
"option_a": "The other 4 matches will fire normally",
"option_b": "The other 4 matches will fire with double the power",
"option_c": "None of the 5 matches will fire",
"option_d": "The panel will explode",
"correct_answer": "C",
"explanation": "In a series circuit, the electrical current must pass completely through one match to get to the next. If any single match in the chain is broken (an open circuit), the entire loop is severed and nothing on that cue will fire."
},
{
"module_name": "Module 3: Display Operations",
"question_text": "When inserting an e-match into the quick match leader of a display shell, what mechanical step is crucial to ensure reliable ignition?",
"option_a": "The match must be left dangling loosely in the pipe",
"option_b": "The match must be fully inserted so the pyrotechnic head is in direct contact with the black match, and then taped or tied securely to prevent it from pulling out",
"option_c": "The match must be glued to the outside of the paper pipe",
"option_d": "The match head must be crushed with pliers before insertion",
"correct_answer": "B",
"explanation": "If the e-match is loose, the concussive 'pop' of the match head can simply blow itself backward out of the quick match pipe without igniting the black powder string. It must be taped or zip-tied tightly into the leader."
},
{
"module_name": "Module 3: Display Operations",
"question_text": "According to NFPA 1123, what is the requirement for the physical location of the Operator in Charge and the main electrical firing panel during the display?",
"option_a": "They must be inside a closed vehicle",
"option_b": "They must be located in a safe area that provides the operator with a direct, unobstructed line of sight to the display area and the fallout zone",
"option_c": "They must be located at least 1,000 feet away regardless of shell size",
"option_d": "They must be located within 10 feet of the mortars to hear them fire",
"correct_answer": "B",
"explanation": "The operator must be able to visually monitor the entire display site, the flight path of the shells, and the fallout area to ensure perimeter security and safety. Firing 'blind' from behind a building or without visual control is strictly prohibited."
},
{
"module_name": "Module 3: Display Operations",
"question_text": "Immediately after the grand finale concludes and the display is officially over, what is the very first mandatory action the operator must take at the firing panel?",
"option_a": "Turn off the panel backlight",
"option_b": "Cheer with the crew",
"option_c": "Turn off the firing panel, remove the safety/arming key, and disconnect the main power source to render the site electrically safe before anyone approaches the mortars",
"option_d": "Test continuity one more time",
"correct_answer": "C",
"explanation": "Before the fire watch or crew can enter the hazard area to look for duds, the system must be completely de-energized. The operator removes the key and disconnects power so an accidental button press cannot fire a delayed shell while the crew is downrange."
},
{
"module_name": "Module 3: Display Operations",
"question_text": "When a crew member is attaching the e-match wires to the field firing module (slat) located near the loaded mortars, what is the safest body position for the technician?",
"option_a": "Straddling the mortar tube to get a good grip on the wires",
"option_b": "Leaning directly over the mouth of the mortars to see the connection clearly",
"option_c": "Kneeling or standing to the side, ensuring no part of their body or head is ever directly over the muzzle or in the trajectory path of the loaded mortars",
"option_d": "Sitting on top of the mortar rack",
"correct_answer": "C",
"explanation": "If an accidental ignition occurs while wiring, the shell will launch instantly. A technician's body must never cross the 'line of fire' (the muzzle of the tube) during any part of the setup process."
},
{
"module_name": "Module 3: Display Operations",
"question_text": "During the post-show inspection, the crew finds a 6-inch shell that failed to fire on an electrical cue. Before attempting to extract the shell from the mortar, what must be done to the e-match?",
"option_a": "Cut the wires with steel scissors",
"option_b": "Leave it connected to the slat just in case",
"option_c": "Disconnect the leg wires from the firing module (slat) and immediately twist them together (shunt them) to protect against stray electrical currents",
"option_d": "Hook it up to a 9-volt battery to safely destroy it",
"correct_answer": "C",
"explanation": "A dud shell with an intact e-match is highly dangerous. The first step in rendering it safe is to disconnect it from the firing system and shunt the leg wires, closing the circuit so stray static cannot accidentally ignite the shell during removal."
},
{
"module_name": "Module 3: Display Operations",
"question_text": "Why is it extremely dangerous to use a standard hardware store multi-meter to test the continuity or resistance of an electric match?",
"option_a": "Multi-meters are not accurate enough",
"option_b": "Multi-meters cannot read pyrotechnic resistance",
"option_c": "Standard multi-meters often output an electrical current that exceeds the 'no-fire' threshold of an electric match, which will instantly detonate the match during testing",
"option_d": "Multi-meters drain the match of its static charge",
"correct_answer": "C",
"explanation": "Electric matches are highly sensitive. Pyrotechnic firing systems use specialized, current-limited testing circuits (usually under 50mA). Standard commercial multi-meters push too much current and can easily fire the match while you are holding it."
},
{
"module_name": "Module 3: Display Operations",
"question_text": "What physical feature is built into almost all modern wireless/RF pyrotechnic firing field modules to ensure crew safety during loading?",
"option_a": "A waterproof speaker",
"option_b": "A physical safety interlock, such as a shunt plug or a physical key switch on the module itself, which completely disconnects the firing capacitor from the cue outputs",
"option_c": "A biometric fingerprint scanner",
"option_d": "A GPS tracker",
"correct_answer": "B",
"explanation": "Because wireless modules cannot be unplugged from a central wire, they must have a physical, mechanical way to isolate the firing power. This is usually a safety key on the module itself that the crew holds while wiring, ensuring the module cannot fire even if commanded to by the remote."
},
{
"module_name": "Module 3: Display Operations",
"question_text": "When an operator is planning a large display with wire runs exceeding 500 feet, why is it critical to calculate the wire gauge (thickness) of the scab wire?",
"option_a": "Thick wire is more likely to be cut by shrapnel",
"option_b": "Long runs of thin wire (high gauge) create excessive electrical resistance, which can cause a massive voltage drop, resulting in the panel failing to deliver enough current to fire the e-match",
"option_c": "Long wires create too much static electricity",
"option_d": "Thin wire burns too brightly",
"correct_answer": "B",
"explanation": "Wire has natural electrical resistance. The longer and thinner the wire, the higher the resistance. If the resistance is too high, Ohm's law dictates the current will drop below the threshold needed to heat the bridge wire, resulting in a failed cue."
},
{
"module_name": "Module 3: Display Operations",
"question_text": "What does the term 'No-Fire Current' mean in the specifications of an electric match?",
"option_a": "The current required to melt the wire completely",
"option_b": "The minimum current required to guarantee the match will ignite 100% of the time",
"option_c": "The maximum continuous electrical current that can be applied to the match for a specified duration without causing it to ignite",
"option_d": "The current produced by the match when it burns",
"correct_answer": "C",
"explanation": "The 'no-fire' current is a safety threshold. It tells the manufacturer of the firing system exactly how much current they can safely push through the match during a continuity test without risking an accidental ignition."
},
{
"module_name": "Module 3: Display Operations",
"question_text": "If a Pyrotechnic Operator is working in extremely dry, low-humidity conditions, what type of clothing is considered a severe hazard while handling and wiring electric matches?",
"option_a": "100% Cotton clothing",
"option_b": "Flame-retardant treated wool",
"option_c": "Synthetic fabrics like nylon, polyester, or fleece, which aggressively generate and store large electrostatic charges",
"option_d": "Leather jackets",
"correct_answer": "C",
"explanation": "Synthetic clothing acts like a static generator. In dry conditions, pulling a fleece jacket or walking in nylon pants can build up thousands of volts of static electricity on the body, which can easily arc to an e-match and ignite it."
},
{
"module_name": "Module 3: Display Operations",
"question_text": "A pyrotechnic crew is using a capacitive discharge firing system. What is the primary difference between this and a simple battery-box firing system?",
"option_a": "It uses solar power instead of batteries",
"option_b": "It uses internal capacitors to build up and store a massive electrical charge, allowing it to release a high-voltage, high-current pulse capable of firing many matches simultaneously",
"option_c": "It is completely immune to water",
"option_d": "It can only fire one match per hour",
"correct_answer": "B",
"explanation": "Batteries alone often lack the instant 'punch' needed to fire dozens of matches at once (like in a finale). Capacitive discharge systems trickle-charge a capacitor, which then dumps a massive, instantaneous surge of power to guarantee ignition."
},
{
"module_name": "Module 3: Display Operations",
"question_text": "When stripping the insulation off the ends of e-match leg wires to make a connection, what care must be taken regarding the wire strands?",
"option_a": "They must be dipped in acid",
"option_b": "They should be stripped with a knife by scraping toward the match head",
"option_c": "The thin copper strands must not be excessively nicked or broken, as reducing the number of strands increases electrical resistance and creates a weak point that can snap during setup",
"option_d": "They must be tied in a knot",
"correct_answer": "C",
"explanation": "E-match wires are often 22 or 24 gauge. If a technician uses poor stripping technique and cuts half the copper strands, the wire's resistance spikes, and it will likely physically break when pulled or manipulated."
},
{
"module_name": "Module 3: Display Operations",
"question_text": "If an operator unpacks an e-match and notices the pyrotechnic composition bead on the tip is cracked or chipped, exposing the tiny bridge wire underneath, what is the required action?",
"option_a": "Tape the composition back together",
"option_b": "Dip it in water to seal the crack",
"option_c": "Discard the e-match and destroy it safely; a compromised pyrotechnic head may fail to ignite, or may ignite but fail to produce enough flame to light the shell's fuse",
"option_d": "Use it, but give it double the voltage",
"correct_answer": "C",
"explanation": "The bridge wire only gets hot for a fraction of a millisecond. It relies on the intimate contact with the pyrotechnic bead to catch fire. If the bead is cracked or missing, the wire will just melt and break, failing to light the shell."
},
{
"module_name": "Module 3: Display Operations",
"question_text": "Under what circumstance is it permissible to untwist (unshunt) the leg wires of an electric match?",
"option_a": "As soon as the match is removed from its shipping box",
"option_b": "When inserting the match into the shell's quick match leader",
"option_c": "Only immediately prior to connecting the match into the firing system field module or the extension wire",
"option_d": "They should never be unshunted",
"correct_answer": "C",
"explanation": "To maintain maximum protection against static and stray currents, the e-match wires must remain twisted together (shunted) during all handling and loading. They are only untwisted at the very last second when making the electrical connection."
},
{
"module_name": "Module 3: Display Operations",
"question_text": "During an electrically fired display, the primary firing computer freezes and completely loses communication with the field modules, causing the show to halt. What is the operator's safest immediate action?",
